<% content_for :title, "Editor guide" %>
<article class="prose">
  <h1>Editor guide</h1>
  <p class="lede">Start small, cite what you know, and leave uncertainty visible.</p>
  <p>Your invitation grants either site-wide editing or access to one carousel and its figures and seats.
    If invited to create a new carousel, you can create one with just its name and fill in details later.
    One-carousel editors cannot edit shared makers or figure types or transfer figures between carousels.
    Your chosen display name and saved edits appear in public history.
    Before contributing, read and agree to the <%= link_to "contribution terms", contribution_terms_path %>.</p>

  <h2>Your first edit</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Pick one carousel you know. Read its source notes and history before changing it.</li>
    <li>Choose a small task: add a source link, improve a description in your own words, or document one figure.</li>
    <li>Sign in, choose <strong>Edit</strong>, make your change, and save. Saved changes are public immediately; there is no approval queue.</li>
    <li>Check the page and its <strong>History</strong>. <%= link_to "Recent changes", recent_changes_path %> lists activity across the site.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Sources and uncertainty</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Cite the operator, an archive, a publication, or another identifiable source. Include a link and the source date when available.
      For your own observations, say when you visited and what you observed.</li>
    <li>Use Markdown links such as <code>[Operator inventory](https://example.com/)</code> in the relevant notes or source field.
      Write descriptions in your own words; a citation does not give permission to copy someone else's prose or photos.</li>
    <li>Unknown is not zero. Leave unknown counts blank; enter zero only when verified.</li>
    <li>Use <strong>confirmed</strong> for an established seat position and <strong>best reading</strong> for a tentative interpretation.
      Explain the evidence and uncertainty in notes.</li>
    <li>Distinguish current arrangements from historical ones. If sources disagree, record the dates and disagreement instead of guessing.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Figures, seats, and layouts</h2>
  <p>A <strong>figure</strong> is a carved object. A <strong>seat</strong> is a platform position.
    A placement connects them over time. Figures can belong to a carousel without currently occupying a seat.</p>
  <p>A seat code such as <strong>5A</strong> means section 5 at the outer rail. Rows run from A inward;
    sections follow the order figures pass someone standing at the rail. A chariot or tub spanning rows is one seat,
    not several duplicate figures.</p>
  <p>Record only known geometry. Section and row counts can be recorded independently, and rotation can remain unknown.
    To correct an existing chart, choose <strong>Edit</strong> and change <strong>Sections</strong> or
    <strong>Rows</strong> under <strong>Platform</strong>. Increases keep existing seats and figures and add unknown seats.
    Decreases only remove untouched unknown seats; figures, research, placement history, and spanning seats are protected.
    Correct a seat's row span and any affected independent ring research before reducing the row count.
    Ask the administrator before establishing an uncertain layout or making substantial structural changes.</p>
  <p>Assigning an already-seated figure to another seat moves it and leaves its old seat empty.
    Check for an existing figure before creating one. To transfer a figure between carousels,
    remove it from its seat, edit its carousel, and then assign a seat at the destination.</p>
  <p>Site-wide editors who cannot find the correct figure type can choose <strong>Add it first</strong>
    beside Type on the Edit Figure page. After creating the shared type, the same figure form reopens
    with it selected. One-carousel editors cannot change the shared type catalog.</p>
  <p>Auto-filled figures are removable draft placeholders. Replacing one, marking its seat empty
    or unknown, or expanding a chariot across it removes that generated placeholder. This supports
    correcting an all-horse fill to a chariot spanning rows A-B with row C empty. Researched figures
    are protected from automatic removal.</p>
  <p><strong>Published totals</strong> describe what a source reports and require source notes.
    Charts show mapped inventory; the two can differ. Published totals and independent ring research
    do not create figures, seats, or placements. Existing ring research is preserved but is no longer edited
    on the carousel form; administrators can maintain it through CSV. For an estimated chart when no layout
    exists, choose <strong>Create draft layout</strong> and review its provisional preview.
    Never invent individual figures just to match a total.</p>

  <h2 id="carousel-csv">Carousel CSV import and export (admins)</h2>
  <p>On the carousel directory, administrators can choose <strong>Import / export CSV</strong>.
    Download all carousel records and editable carousel fields, including blank fields. Figures, seats,
    placements, and their history are not exported. Back up the database before a substantial import.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Open the UTF-8 CSV as text in a spreadsheet, preserving the headers, line breaks, leading apostrophes,
      <code>csv_format</code>, IDs, slug and <code>updated_at</code>. Avoid automatic date or number reformatting.
      For a new carousel, add a row with blank ID, slug and updated_at. Never copy another row's ID.</li>
    <li>Upload the file or paste CSV, then preview. A smaller file may omit columns, but must include
      <code>name</code>. Exact name matches and other names are flagged as possible duplicates, not merged.
      Review them manually, along with locations and sources.</li>
    <li>Inspect the changes and warnings before choosing <strong>Apply all changes</strong>. Any validation error
      blocks the batch. The preview expires after 30 minutes and can be applied only once. If relevant data
      changes before confirmation, export or preview again.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Blank cells and omitted columns do not change existing values; <code>__CLEAR__</code> clears an optional
    value. To enter that exact text, use <code>\__CLEAR__</code>; double a leading backslash to preserve it.
    No row implies deletion. Zero is a value, not a blank. New imported carousels default to
    <code>unknown</code>; specify <code>operating</code>, <code>stored</code>, or <code>lost</code>
    only when supported by evidence. An existing carousel's status is unchanged when blank.</p>
  <p>For a research spreadsheet with different column names, first map the data to supported headers.
    For example, rename <code>website</code> to <code>official_url</code> only after checking that it is
    the operator's current URL. Incorporate reviewed source links and caveats into the appropriate
    source or body fields; remove research-only columns before importing.</p>
  <p>Use an existing maker ID from the list on the import page. Independent ring columns are
    <code>ring_A_positions_count</code>, <code>ring_A_horses_count</code>, and <code>ring_A_mount</code>,
    repeated through F; mounts are <code>stander</code> or <code>jumper</code>. Sources remain required
    for published counts, provisional layouts, and ring research. Editing known dimensions is prohibited;
    adding both previously unknown dimensions creates unknown seats. No figures or placements are created.</p>
  <p>CSV exports prefix potentially spreadsheet-executable text with an apostrophe; keep it when saving.
    Do not open untrusted CSV files in a spreadsheet without appropriate formula protections.
    Imports are limited to 1 MB, 5,000 rows, and 10,000 newly generated seats; split larger exports
    into smaller CSV files, retaining the header. Each successful import is transactional and records
    the administrator in normal page history. This does not provide a one-click batch undo.</p>

  <h2 id="database-export">Copy the database to development (admins)</h2>
  <p>On the Invitations page, administrators can download a consistent SQLite snapshot of the primary database.
    It contains private accounts, password hashes, sessions, invitation links, and other data. Keep it private,
    do not commit it, and remove downloaded copies securely when no longer needed.</p>
  <p>Stop your local Rails server, jobs, consoles, and other processes accessing the development database.
    Update the local checkout to the same database schema as production, then run:</p>
  <pre><code>bin/rails backups:restore_development FILE=/absolute/path/to/download.sqlite3 CONFIRM=replace-development</code></pre>
  <p>This replaces the development database, saving a backup of the old one in <code>storage/</code>.
    Copied sessions and unaccepted invitations are removed and copied passwords disabled. Set a new local
    password with <code>bin/rails users:reset_password EMAIL=your-admin-email</code> before signing in.
    Keep the old database backup private too. Never use a production password in development.</p>
  <p>The snapshot is the primary database only: uploaded files and the separate job queue, cache, and cable
    databases are not included. Do not expect video attachments to work without their separately stored files.
    Restart local services only after the restore finishes. See the deployment guide for backup details.</p>

  <h2>Mistakes, disagreements, and help</h2>
  <p>For a simple typo, make a corrective edit. For a misplaced figure, duplicate record, layout problem, or uncertain correction,
    pause and ask the administrator rather than repeatedly changing records.
    Admin reverts restore individual updated records; they are not a universal Undo for new records or multi-step moves.</p>
  <p>Be courteous and discuss evidence rather than people. Do not overwrite another editor's work repeatedly during a disagreement;
    the administrator makes the final moderation decision and can suspend access.</p>
  <p>Use your existing conversation with your inviter for help for now.
    See <%= link_to "About / Contribute", about_path(anchor: "contact") %> for the current contact arrangements.</p>
</article>
